PART V — DEVELOP THE NETWORK DIAGNOSTICS

6. STRENGTHEN THE EXISTING DNS ENGINE

Improve the DNS implementation and connect it more intelligently to the AI Operator.

The platform should support the available DNS operations through existing drivers, including appropriate A, AAAA, CNAME, MX, TXT, NS, and other implemented record queries.

For each request, report actual results where available:

Queried domain.

Requested record type.

Returned records.

Resolver information.

Measured query duration.

Error code and error details.

Whether the operation used the DNS protocol or the operating system's name-resolution service.

Distinguish these situations:

Domain not found.

Record type has no returned data.

Resolver unavailable.

DNS timeout.

Invalid hostname.

Network failure.

Local hosts-file resolution.

Successful response.

Do not describe every DNS error as NXDOMAIN. Do not invent an IP address when resolution fails.

Do not assume that a public resolver such as Google DNS is configured merely because the application requires a default.

Use the real configured resolver or OS resolver, and label the method accurately.

Avoid DNS resolution paths that can unexpectedly block for tens of seconds.

Implement bounded asynchronous resolution and consistent timeout behavior. Ensure that cached data is distinguishable from a fresh lookup.

Intelligent DNS behavior

When I ask "Check DNS connectivity," inspect the actual local DNS configuration and perform a sensible health check.

When I ask "Check DNS for this hostname," resolve the requested hostname.

These are different operations and must not be confused.

A DNS-health request should not automatically inherit an unrelated hostname from a previous conversation.

If the requested hostname resembles a known typo, use a cautious correction strategy. Automatically correct only sufficiently unambiguous cases and show the correction to the user. For uncertain cases, ask a short clarification question.

Never silently substitute an arbitrary internal or customer domain.

7. STRENGTHEN PING AND TCP DIAGNOSTICS

Improve the existing ICMP and TCP implementations.

Ping

Report actual packet counts, received replies, loss percentage, and measured RTT statistics where available.

Handle ICMP restrictions honestly.

If ICMP fails but a permitted TCP connection succeeds, explain that the service is reachable over TCP even though ping did not receive a reply.

Do not manufacture a successful ping from a TCP handshake. The two measurements must remain distinct.

If a runtime sandbox prevents raw ICMP, identify the limitation and provide an optional alternative connectivity check when appropriate.

TCP ports

Support requests involving one or multiple ports using the existing port diagnostic engine.

Distinguish:

A successful TCP handshake.

An explicit connection refusal.

A timeout.

A DNS resolution error.

A local permission or execution error.

An unsupported operation.

Use the actual measured handshake duration. Remove arbitrary banner-wait delays when they are not required for the test itself.

Do not claim that a service is healthy simply because a port accepts connections. Port openness establishes transport connectivity, not application health.

The system should understand short follow-ups such as:

"Try 80 too."

"Now check 443."

"Test the other port."

"Repeat the same check."

Resolve these references through reliable session context, not through blind reuse of an old target.

8. DEVELOP WEBSITE AND HTTPS DIAGNOSTICS

Extend the existing website and cross-layer diagnostic path.

For a request to test a website, execute the relevant supported checks:

Resolve the hostname.

Establish TCP connectivity to the relevant service port.

Inspect the TLS handshake and certificate validation for HTTPS.

Obtain an HTTP response.

Correlate the evidence.

Do not claim that every layer succeeded unless every required check actually ran and returned sufficient evidence.

Handle HTTP redirects correctly. A 301 or 302 response indicates redirection, not necessarily a failure.

Handle HTTP 4xx and 5xx responses as application-layer evidence with the correct interpretation.

For example:

DNS failure means the hostname could not be resolved by the tested resolver.

TCP refusal indicates that the attempted connection was refused.

TCP timeout means no definitive handshake result was obtained.

TLS handshake failure identifies a TLS-layer problem only to the extent demonstrated by the evidence.

Certificate validation failure must be distinguished from a completed TLS handshake.

HTTP 500 indicates that an HTTP server responded with an application-level error.

HTTP 200 establishes a successful response for the tested request, not universal application health.

Correct all mismatched result fields so TLS protocol information, cipher details, certificate status, HTTP timing, and response details are read from the real schemas returned by the drivers.

Never show [object Object], undefinedms, or invented numbers in the user interface.

When a field is unavailable, display that fact clearly.

