PART VII — IMPROVE AUTONOMOUS INVESTIGATION

11. DEVELOP A BOUNDED INVESTIGATION PLANNER

Extend the existing AI Operator and investigation engine so that multi-step troubleshooting is systematic, efficient, and explainable.

When the user reports a problem, the AI should:

Define the problem in practical terms.

Identify the relevant target and environment.

Determine what evidence is already available.

Build a short initial diagnostic plan.

Execute the most informative low-risk checks.

Compare the results.

Identify the earliest confirmed failure.

Determine which uncertainties remain.

Run additional checks only when they can improve the diagnosis.

Stop when the evidence is sufficient or the investigation reaches its defined budget.

Deliver a concise conclusion and the next useful action.

Do not launch every subsystem for every problem.

For a basic connectivity problem, begin with the relevant checks. For a complex incident, expand the investigation when the evidence justifies it.

Example investigation: website unreachable

An intelligent investigation may examine:

Whether local host network information can be read.

Whether a default route is verified.

Whether DNS resolution succeeds.

Whether the target is permitted by the security policy.

Whether a TCP connection can be established.

Whether the TLS handshake and certificate validation succeed.

Whether an HTTP response is returned.

However, do not label unverified telemetry as healthy merely to fill every step in a report.

If a local NIC check is unavailable, identify that fact. If gateway telemetry is missing, do not invent a gateway address.

If a probe fails before reaching a later layer, report the dependency accurately and run only independent checks that can still offer useful evidence.

Confidence and uncertainty

Develop a disciplined evidence-based reasoning layer.

The AI should differentiate:

Directly observed facts.

Correlated evidence.

Plausible hypotheses.

Unsupported possibilities.

Verified root causes.

Do not calculate arbitrary confidence percentages without an established and validated scoring method.

Do not call a problem solved because the explanation sounds plausible.

A root cause should be considered confirmed only when the available evidence meets the relevant criteria.

